<title>BELGIUM: Siemens wins Belgian tram order.</title>
<headline>Siemens wins Belgian tram order.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1996-10-24</dateline>
<text>
<p>A consortium led by Germany's Siemens AG has won a three billion Belgian franc order for 45 trams for a number of Belgian cities from transport company  De Lijn, Belgian newspapers said on Thursday.</p>
<p>The German consortium includes Adtranz, Duewag and  Deutsche Waggonbau.  </p>
<p>The first nine single cab trams are for the city of Antwerp and are due to be delivered at the end of 1998 or early 1999.</p>
<p>The papers said the Siemens consortium beat a group made up of  Eurorail Bombardier, based in the city of Brugge, and Charleroi-based  Acec, and a group formed by Swiss  Vevey, Dutch  Holec and Spain's  CAF and Belgian  Jonckheere.</p>
<p>The papers said the Siemens consortium had met all the specifications and also beat the other two groups on price.</p>
